==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|nimman|lang=ang}}, probably from {{proto|Indo-European|nem-|take|lang=ang}}.

Germanic cognates include Old High German {{term|neman|lang=goh}} (German {{term|nehmen|lang=de}}), Old Saxon {{term|niman|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|nemen|lang=nl}}), Old Norse {{term|nema|lang=non}} (Danish {{term|nemme|lang=da}}), Gothic {{term|𐌽𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌽|tr=niman|lang=got|sc=Goth}}.  The Indo-European root was also the source of Old Irish {{term|nem||present|lang=sga}}, Latvian {{term|nams|lang=lv}}, Ancient Greek {{term|νέμω|tr=nemō|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈnɪmɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=IV|}} (''3 singular present'' '''[[nimþ]]''', ''3 singular preterite'' '''[[nam]]''', ''preterite plural'' '''[[namon|nāmon]]''', ''past participle'' (ġe)'''[[numen]]''')

# to [[take]], [[seize]]

